    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d481">

                <pb facs="0737" n="621" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg621"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.003 C 73/41</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chinese Minister</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Sze</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-05-11"><hi rendition="#italic">May 11,
                        1923</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your Legation’s note of May 4, 1923, advising me of the readiness of your
                    Government to have the Commission on Extraterritoriality in China, provided for
                    in the Resolution adopted on December 10, 1921, by the Washington Conference on
                    the Limitation of Armament, meet at Peking on November 1, 1923.</p>
                <p>In reply I have the honor to inform you that the meeting of the Commission at
                    Peking at the time stated will be very agreeable to this Government, and that I
                    have, by way of compliance with the request made in the note, instructed the
                    appropriate American diplomatic officers to communicate copies of the note to
                    all the Governments concerned.<anchor n="60" xml:id="d481-f1"/></p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles E. Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d481-f1" n="60" xml:id="d481-f1-content">
                <p>Copies of the note were mailed to the appropriate American diplomatic
                    representatives on May 11, and, on the same date, a circular telegram was sent
                    instructing that the Foreign Offices be informed that the Chinese Government had
                    indicated that it would be agreeable to have the Commission on
                    Extraterritoriality meet at Peking Nov. 1, 1923.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
